%META:TOPICPARENT{name="WebHome"}%
---+ Developers

%TOC%

This page is the on-ramp for anyone who wants to hack on CGV Web, run a
private instance, regenerate the calorimeter geometry or rebuild the
Rust-to-WASM ATLAS-ID parser. End-users should follow
[[GettingStarted][Getting Started]] instead — the hosted viewer at
[[https://nipscern.com/projects/cgvweb][nipscern.com/projects/cgvweb]]
needs no install.

---++ Just run the viewer

The repository does NOT ship the compiled geometry or the JiveXML
samples; they live on the GitHub Release =geometry-v4= and are fetched
by =npm run fetch:geometry= and =npm run fetch:samples=. The Rust
parser WASM is committed under =public/parser/pkg/=.

For a quick local run:

<verbatim>
git clone https://github.com/nipscernlab/cgv-web.git
cd cgv-web
npm install --ignore-scripts
npm run fetch:geometry     # downloads CaloGeometry.glb.gz (~5 MB)
npm run fetch:samples      # downloads default_xml/*.xml
python serve.py            # starts the backend on 127.0.0.1:8080
</verbatim>

Open =http://localhost:8080/=. =serve.py= is a small ThreadingHTTPServer
that serves =public/= and exposes =/api/xml/*= for the SERVER sub-mode.
Setting =XML_FOLDER= before launching points the SERVER mode at a local
folder for first-paint:

<verbatim>
XML_FOLDER="$PWD/public/default_xml" python serve.py
</verbatim>

---++ Full build pipeline

Rebuilding is only needed if you:

   * regenerate =CaloGeometry.glb= from a new =.root= file, or
   * touch the Rust parser and need to recompile the WASM.

---+++ Required tools

| *Tool*        | *Version* | *Purpose*                                   |
| Node.js        | ≥ 18      | Runs the =.root= → =.glb= pipeline          |
| npm            | bundled   | Installs JS dependencies                    |
| Rust           | stable    | Compiles the ATLAS-ID parser                |
| =wasm-pack=    | ≥ 0.12    | Builds the parser to WebAssembly            |

Install Rust and wasm-pack:

<verbatim>
# Linux / macOS
curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh
cargo install wasm-pack

# Windows
# download rustup from https://www.rust-lang.org/tools/install
cargo install wasm-pack
</verbatim>

---+++ Step-by-step (Linux / macOS)

   1 Install Node dependencies from the project root:
<verbatim>
npm install --ignore-scripts
</verbatim>
   =--ignore-scripts= is required because =jsroot= ships postinstall hooks
   that CGV Web does not need.
   1 Patch the =jsroot= geometry modules (=geobase.mjs=, =csg.mjs=) so they
   resolve outside the =jsroot= tree:
<verbatim>
cd setup
node setup.mjs
cd ..
</verbatim>
   1 Place the source geometry at =public/geometry_data/CaloGeometry.root= and
   compile it to optimised glTF-binary:
<verbatim>
node tools/setup/root2scene.mjs public/geometry_data/CaloGeometry.root --out public/geometry_data
</verbatim>
   1 Strip and quantise the GLB (removes unused UVs / tangents / materials):
<verbatim>
node tools/setup/optimize_glb.mjs --quantize
mv public/geometry_data/CaloGeometry_opt.glb public/geometry_data/CaloGeometry.glb
</verbatim>
   Typical reduction: ~330 MB → ~70 MB, visually identical. Bump
   =GEO_CACHE_VER= in =public/index.html= to force client cache refresh.
   1 Build the Rust ATLAS-ID parser to WASM:
<verbatim>
cd parser
wasm-pack build --target web --release
cd ..
</verbatim>
   Output: =public/parser/pkg/atlas_id_parser.js= and =atlas_id_parser_bg.wasm=,
   loaded directly by =public/js/main.js=.

---+++ One-shot Windows build — =build.bat=

A =build.bat= at the project root chains every step above and is the
recommended entry point on Windows. Double-click it, or run from a
terminal:

<verbatim>
cd C:\path\to\cgv-web
build.bat
</verbatim>

The script performs these stages:

| *Step* | *Action*                                                                         |
| 0      | Check for =node=, =npm=, =rustc=, =wasm-pack= (installs =wasm-pack= if missing)   |
| 1      | =npm install --ignore-scripts=                                                   |
| 2      | =node tools/setup/setup.mjs= — patch =jsroot= geometry modules                   |
| 3      | Delete any stale =public/geometry_data/CaloGeometry.glb= and =.glb.gz=          |
| 4      | Verify =public/geometry_data/CaloGeometry.root= exists                          |
| 5      | =node tools/setup/root2scene.mjs= — compile =.root= → optimised =.glb=           |
| 6      | Compress the =.glb= to =.glb.gz= with PowerShell's =GZipStream=                  |
| 7      | =wasm-pack build --target web --release= — build the Rust parser                 |

On success the end state is:

<verbatim>
public\geometry_data\CaloGeometry.glb         (optimised + quantised)
public\geometry_data\CaloGeometry.glb.gz      (gzip, shipped to clients)
public\parser\pkg\atlas_id_parser.js
public\parser\pkg\atlas_id_parser_bg.wasm
</verbatim>

Any step failing aborts the script with a non-zero exit code and a
human-readable message. Run it from an elevated shell if =cargo install=
needs administrative privileges to place =wasm-pack= on =PATH=.

---++ Project layout

<verbatim>
cgv-web/
├── public/                     everything served to the browser (deploy artifact)
│   ├── index.html              entry point
│   ├── css/                    modular stylesheets
│   ├── js/                     viewer logic split by concern
│   │   ├── main.js             top-level wiring
│   │   ├── modes/              liveMode, serverMode, sampleMode
│   │   ├── particles/          tracks, clusters, jets, photons, electrons, muons, taus
│   │   ├── overlays/           hits, MET, vertex
│   │   ├── parsers/            jet, muon, tau, hits, MET, vertex parsers
│   │   ├── visibility/         per-class show / hide, FCAL renderer, layer gates
│   │   ├── cinema/             scripted tour path
│   │   ├── bootstrap/          UI wiring (topToolbar, layersPanel, helpersPanel, ...)
│   │   ├── i18n/               translation tables (en, fr, pt, no)
│   │   └── ...                 (minimap, slicer, screenshot, statusHud, etc.)
│   ├── assets/                 icons, fonts, branding, images
│   ├── vendor/                 Three.js, jsroot, icon fonts
│   ├── default_xml/            JiveXML samples + index.json (fetched, not committed)
│   ├── geometry_data/          CaloGeometry.glb.gz (fetched, not committed)
│   ├── parser/pkg/             wasm-pack output (committed; loaded by main.js)
│   ├── live_atlas/             ATLAS Live polling client
│   └── twiki/                  this TWiki bundle, served at /cgv-web/twiki/
├── parser/                     Rust ATLAS-ID parser source
│   ├── src/                    lib.rs + xml_parser.rs
│   └── Cargo.toml
├── tests/                      Vitest unit tests
├── docs/                       LINUX_BUILD.md, geo_decoder.pdf, ...
├── tools/                      offline tooling, not deployed
│   ├── setup/                  jsroot patches, root2scene compiler
│   └── scripts/                fetch-geometry, fetch-samples, deploy
├── server.py                   systemd-launched production wrapper
├── serve.py                    HTTP server + /api/xml/* backend
├── cgv-web.spec                RPM spec (Alma / Rocky / RHEL 9)
├── build-rpm.sh                one-stop RPM build on Linux
└── build.bat                   one-stop Windows frontend build
</verbatim>

---++ Code conventions

   * No bundler — =public/index.html= loads =public/js/main.js= directly as a classic script, =tools/twiki/app.js= as an ES module. Keep imports browser-native.
   * =public/js/main.js= is a single file. Split only when a standalone concern emerges (WASM adapter, i18n table).
   * The WASM parser is called off the main thread via dynamic =import()=; never block the render loop on decoding.
   * Translations live in the =TRANSLATIONS= map at the top of =public/js/main.js=. Every UI string referenced by a =data-i18n= attribute must exist in all four locales (EN, FR, PT, NO).
   * Screenshots must hide transient UI: the slicer wedge gizmo, hover outlines and the cursor crosshair are suppressed during capture (see =renderAndDownload= in =public/js/main.js=).

---++ Previewing the TWiki locally

The TWiki bundle is shipped inside the runtime at =public/twiki/=. The
"Technical TWiki" link in the sidebar of the viewer resolves to this
folder, so any local =serve.py= or static HTTP server pointed at =public/=
also serves the TWiki at =/twiki/=. See =public/twiki/PUBLISHING.txt= for
the procedure to copy each =.twiki= file into the CERN ATLAS TWiki.

---++ Building the RPM (Alma / Rocky / RHEL 9)

A =build-rpm.sh= at the repository root drives the full pipeline on
Linux: it installs system packages, Node.js, Rust + wasm-pack, downloads
geometry and samples, builds the WASM parser, stages a runtime-only tree
(no =node_modules=, no =.root= inputs, no =tools/=, no =nipscern/= baker)
and runs =rpmbuild=. Interactive version prompt, or pass the version on
the CLI:

<verbatim>
./build-rpm.sh                  # interactive, builds only
./build-rpm.sh 2.3.0            # non-interactive
./build-rpm.sh 2.3.0 --install  # build + install locally + smoke test
</verbatim>

The Apache configuration is owned by the host (Puppet at P1); the RPM
does not ship =/etc/httpd/conf.d/cgv-web.conf= and does not touch
=httpd= in =%post=. A reference snippet ships at
=/var/www/cgv-web/examples/apache-cgv-web.conf.example= for comparison
with the host's setup.

See =docs/LINUX_BUILD.md= for the manual fallback (every command from a
fresh box to the upload on lxplus and the message template for the P1
sysadmins).

---++ Opening an issue / contributing

   * Source — [[https://github.com/nipscernlab/cgv-web][github.com/nipscern/cgv-web]]
   * Issues — [[https://github.com/nipscernlab/cgv-web/issues][github.com/nipscern/cgv-web/issues]]
   * Extended dev notes — [[https://nipscern.com/projects/cgv][nipscern.com/projects/cgv]]

Pull requests welcome. Follow the existing code style, keep build
artefacts out of the diff (the repo already commits pre-built =pkg/= and
=CaloGeometry.glb.gz=), and run =build.bat= (or its per-step
equivalents) before opening the PR so reviewers see the regenerated
artefacts in one commit.

